<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<FinalDraft DocumentType="Script" Template="No" Version="5">
<Content>
<Paragraph Number="1" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. OPEN SEA - NIGHT (1999)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>FADE IN:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Black water. Lightning, and we see it: a sea the colour of engine oil, running in long hills.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>AERIAL: ELEVEN FISHING BOATS SCATTERED ACROSS THE SWELL, MASTHEAD LIGHTS SWINGING. SOME OF THEM ARE TOO FAR APART.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The LITTLE FLOWER, a wooden thirty-footer with her name in blue on the bow, climbs a wave and drops off the back of it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>In the wheelhouse VARGHESE PILLAI (48), bare-chested under an oilskin, holds the wheel with both arms. His DECKHAND bails with a cut plastic can.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A ship-to-shore set is bolted above the compass, its dial glowing orange. Through the hiss, a woman&apos;s voice, unhurried, almost amused.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (ON RADIO)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kadalkkara to all boats. Wind west-southwest, forty knots and gusting. Put your bow into it and don&apos;t argue with it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VARGHESE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(keying the handset)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kadalkkara, Little Flower. We hear you. Where is the Seagull?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (ON RADIO)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(a beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Seagull, Seagull, Kadalkkara. Hamza, give me a light. Give me anything.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nothing but sea noise.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (ON RADIO) (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Little Flower, I&apos;ll read you the river mouth. Have your pencil ready, Varghese. I know you never have your pencil ready.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Varghese almost smiles. He fishes a pencil stub out of the oilskin and wets it on his tongue.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (ON RADIO) (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nine degrees, fifty...</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>CLOSE ON THE SET&apos;S DIAL. THE NEEDLE SHIVERS, DROPS, LIFTS, DROPS.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Her voice tears into static. Varghese slaps the set.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VARGHESE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into the handset)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Mariamma. Say again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A wave comes over the bow, white and enormous, and fills the frame.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="2" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. COAST RADIO STATION - OPERATIONS ROOM - NIGHT (1999)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A concrete room built for radios. Two transmitter racks, valves glowing behind wire mesh. A steel desk, two microphones, two handsets. A Nagra tape recorder, its quarter-inch reel turning slowly. A wall chart of the coast with pins for boats. The clock says 23:38. Rain hits the shutters like thrown gravel.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>MARIAMMA THOMAS (38) works both sets at once, a handset at each ear, marking positions on the chart with a grease pencil. Her wristwatch stands propped against the microphone base where she can see it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into the left handset)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Morning Star, Kadalkkara. I have you. Stay on this channel.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into the right)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Little Flower, are you back with me?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Under the desk, on a folded fertiliser sack, YOUNG ANJALI (11) sleeps in her school pinafore, a transistor radio on her chest.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>ANGLE ON YOUNG ANJALI. HER EYES OPEN. SHE LISTENS TO HER MOTHER&apos;S FEET ON THE CONCRETE.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>YOUNG ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Amma. Is it the big one?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&apos;s a normal one. Sleep, or I&apos;ll put you on the air and you can read the weather.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Young Anjali smiles and does not close her eyes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The door opens. Wind lifts the papers off the desk. JOSEPH D&apos;CRUZ, &quot;JOE&quot;, the station chief, younger and thinner than we will know him, stands in the doorway, soaked, holding a telegram in a plastic sleeve.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Mariamma. It&apos;s from the Port.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(not looking)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then read it to the Port. I&apos;ve got Morning Star in one ear and nobody in the other.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Joe looks at her. He looks at the child under the desk. He steps back and pulls the door shut.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The clock moves to 23:40.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Every light in the room dies at once. The valves fade to nothing. Total black. Only the rain.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>In the dark, one small sound: the Nagra, still turning on its own batteries.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>YOUNG ANJALI (O.S.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Amma?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>SMASH CUT TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="3" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. SOUNDWAVE STUDIO - BENGALURU - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Silence of another kind. A windowless edit suite, grey foam, a wall of monitors.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>ANJALI THOMAS (34) sits too close to the screen in headphones. On her wrist, an old ladies&apos; watch with a cracked crystal. It says 23:52. It always says 23:52.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>CLOSE ON THE WAVEFORM: A WOMAN&apos;S VOICE, ZOOMED IN UNTIL EACH BREATH IS A SMALL GREY HILL.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali selects a breath. Deletes it. Crossfades. On the speakers a voice actor says &quot;Whiter than your mother&apos;s whites,&quot; for clearly the fortieth time.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>NIKHIL (40), producer, branded fleece, comes in with two coffees and gives her neither.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>STUDIO PRODUCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Client says she sounds tired.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She is tired. She did it forty times.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>STUDIO PRODUCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>So make her not tired. You&apos;re the magician.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I take things out. That&apos;s the job.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(she plays it back: smooth, airless)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There. Nobody breathes in Bengaluru.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>STUDIO PRODUCER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>By six?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>By five, if you leave.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He leaves. Her phone buzzes face down on the desk. She ignores it. It buzzes again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>INSERT: THE PHONE SCREEN. FATHIMA. THREE MISSED CALLS.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She answers.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA (ON PHONE)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Finally. Appachan walked out in the night. The door was open, his chappals still inside. We&apos;ve been looking since four.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali pulls the headphones off. The client&apos;s voice keeps going, tiny, from the desk.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Where have you looked?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA (ON PHONE)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The church. The harbour. The bus stand. Kannan&apos;s gone too, so at least he has the dog.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Latha says try the station.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The station&apos;s locked.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA (ON PHONE)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He has the keys round his neck, Anju. Twenty years round his neck.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And they&apos;re knocking it down. There&apos;s a notice. Next week.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>On the monitor the waveform sits frozen, every breath removed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;ll get the night bus.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA (ON PHONE)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(surprised)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You will?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Call me if he turns up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She hangs up before Fathima can say anything else.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="4" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. KSRTC BUS - MOVING - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A red KSRTC bus on the highway south. Rain runs sideways along the windows. Passengers asleep with their mouths open.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali, knees up in a window seat, takes a yellow Walkman out of her bag. It is held shut with a rubber band.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>INSERT: THE CASSETTE LABEL, IN BLUE BALLPOINT: KDK WEATHER, AUG 98, AMMA.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She puts on the foam earphones and presses play. A four-note jingle on a toy organ, wobbling on old tape.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(on the cassette)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Good morning, Kadalkkara. This is Mariamma at the coast station with the weather for the boats. Wind northwest, ten knots, swell one metre, low tide at five forty...</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(a rustle of paper)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No. Five fourteen. Sorry, Raghavan, don&apos;t come and shout at me...</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She starts to laugh, helpless, on air.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali presses stop before the laugh ends. She looks at the rain. She looks at the watch. 23:52.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>DISSOLVE TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="5" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. COAST RADIO STATION - DAWN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>WIDE ON THE STATION: A FLAT CONCRETE BOX ON A DUNE, A RUSTED LATTICE MAST TWICE ITS HEIGHT, COCONUT PALMS LEANING AWAY FROM THE SEA.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Demolition tape wound round the chain-link fence. A sun-bleached Port Trust board. Crows along the mast stays.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>An autorickshaw pulls away down the sand track and leaves Anjali standing with her bag.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Inside the fence, at the foot of the mast, APPACHAN (71) sits with his back to the steel. White stubble, a wet mundu, a vest. Round his neck, on a coir string, a ring of old iron keys. A brown village dog, KANNAN, lies across his feet like a sandbag.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Outside the fence, a white police jeep. INSPECTOR LATHA MENON (45), khaki, sleeves rolled, drinks tea from a steel tumbler and does not hurry.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You came on the night bus.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>How do you know?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You have the face.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(she nods at the fence)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Three in the morning he climbed that. Seventy-one. My constables can&apos;t climb that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is he hurt?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He&apos;s wet. He says he&apos;s waiting for the shift to finish.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali ducks through a gap where the mesh has been peeled back from a post. Kannan thumps his tail once and stays put.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Appachan.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He looks at her. Something works behind his eyes and does not find its place.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You&apos;re late. She&apos;s been on since six. She hasn&apos;t eaten.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali crouches and takes his hand. It is cold and gritty.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then let&apos;s go home and eat. All of us.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He looks up at the mast. The crows shift.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&apos;s very quiet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(looking up too)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Yes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Latha tips the rest of her tea onto the sand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;ll drive you. The dog goes in the back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="6" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. THOMAS HOUSE - KITCHEN - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A small kitchen with a red oxide floor, a wood stove and a gas ring side by side, a calendar stuck on a month long gone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>FATHIMA BEEVI (33), headscarf pushed back, boils milk and tea leaves in a dented pan, then pulls the tea from one tumbler to the other at arm&apos;s length, a long brown rope of it in the air.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(from the doorway)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&apos;s how she did it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who do you think taught me? I stood on a stool.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Appachan, dry now, eats rice and fish curry with his fingers. Kannan waits under his chair.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sainaba. More fish.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fathima gives him more fish without blinking.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(quietly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sainaba was my mother. Some days I&apos;m her. Yesterday I was the Bishop.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>How long has he been like this?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Two years. You&apos;d know if you rang on a day that wasn&apos;t Christmas.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It lands. Anjali takes it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>On the fridge, under a mango-shaped magnet, a typed notice.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>INSERT: THE NOTICE. PORT TRUST, KADALKKARA DIVISION. DEMOLITION OF THE DISUSED COAST RADIO STATION WILL COMMENCE SEVEN DAYS FROM THE DATE OF THIS NOTICE.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Seven days.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A man came with a clipboard. Suresh Pillai.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Varghese chettan&apos;s son.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He works for the contractor now. He&apos;s very happy about it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(not looking up)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nobody is happy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They both look at him. He goes on eating.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(at the door)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The radio club meets at the shop tonight. Stay a few days, Anju. Not for him. For me.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She goes. Appachan pushes the fish plate toward Anjali. She takes a piece.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="7" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. KADALKKARA HARBOUR - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Noon. Diesel, gulls, crows working the fish scraps, an ice truck reversing with its horn going. Men fold wet nets in long pleats. Boats lie rafted three deep along the wharf: Bismillah, Sea Queen, Ambadi, Jyothi.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali walks through it, a stranger who still knows where every puddle is.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>An OLD FISHERMAN mending a net looks up. He stands.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>OLD FISHERMAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Mariamma&apos;s girl.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He touches her cheek with two rough fingers, as if to check she is real.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>OLD FISHERMAN (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Five fourteen. Low tide. She got it wrong on the radio and laughed. I&apos;m Raghavan. The one she told not to shout at her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;ve got that tape.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>OLD FISHERMAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then you&apos;ve got more than me.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(he looks up at the mast on the dune)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That night I was on the Jyothi. We followed Varghese&apos;s light in. We never heard her. Not one word.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He sits and goes back to his net.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali climbs the track to the station fence. A SURVEY CREW in hard hats sprays orange crosses and numbers on the walls. A theodolite stands in the sand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>SURESH PILLAI (52), white shirt, contractor&apos;s lanyard, reading glasses on a cord, holds a clipboard. He knows exactly who she is.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The fence is there for a reason.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;m on the outside of it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Today.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to the crew)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Mark the mast footings too.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You sold us ice candy at the church fair.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And you took two and paid for one.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I was eleven.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I heard about your grandfather. I&apos;m sorry. There&apos;s a guard on the gate from tonight. For his safety.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is anything still inside?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Bats. Rust. Asbestos.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(he finally looks at her)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The building is coming down, Anjali. And so is the story. This village has walked past that thing for twenty-three years. That&apos;s enough.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He turns back to his clipboard. Anjali looks up at the orange cross on the operations room window.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="8" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. PORT TRUST OFFICE - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Ceiling fans, green steel cupboards, files tied with red tape in towers on every desk.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Across a desk from Anjali sits MR. KRISHNADAS (50s), Deputy Traffic Manager, a man who has said no so often it has become a kind of manners.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PORT OFFICER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Madam, the building is structurally unsafe. It is in the engineer&apos;s report.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I don&apos;t want to live in it. I want one hour.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PORT OFFICER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The land is leased to the contractor. It is not mine to give you an hour of.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>My mother worked there eleven years. Her things...</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PORT OFFICER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Station records went to the regional office in 2004. What is left is debris.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(he slides a form across)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Form 14, for copies. Six to eight weeks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&apos;ll be a car park in six to eight weeks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PORT OFFICER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A container yard.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(a little sorry)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Not my decision, madam.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the next desk, back half turned, Suresh waits for a stamp on his site schedule. He has heard all of it. Krishnadas stamps the schedule twice. Suresh signs it, caps his pen and stands.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Passing behind Anjali&apos;s chair he slows for a second. Then he keeps going.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She folds the form in half, and in half again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>CUT TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="9" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. FATHIMA&apos;S TEA SHOP - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A tea shop on the harbour road: a glass case of banana fritters, a copper urn hissing, a fluorescent tube with moths.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rain ticks on the tin roof. Inside, six radios are tuned to six different stations at once.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>After closing. Six KIDS from the school radio club crowd a bench around an old shortwave receiver with a coat-hanger aerial. RAHUL (16), skinny, a soldering iron behind his ear like a pencil, turns the dial like a safecracker. MEENU (12), in a uniform two sizes too big, writes in an exercise book.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MEENU</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&apos;s Sri Lanka. Write Sri Lanka.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&apos;s a boat from Kollam asking for cigarettes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali sits at the corner table with tea she is not drinking. Fathima wipes the counter.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Rahul. Show her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(not looking up)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She doesn&apos;t want to see.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She fixes sound for a living. Her headphones cost more than this shop.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rahul looks at Anjali. Then he brings out from under the bench a round blue biscuit tin. A whip aerial made from a curtain rod sticks out of the lid. The switch is a ceiling fan regulator.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&apos;s a transmitter. Medium wave. One watt, maybe two. We want to read the weather for the boats. Like...</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(he stops)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Like before.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali lifts the lid. A nest of wires, a transistor bolted to the tin, a great deal of solder.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>This will set the shop on fire.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It did. Last month.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MEENU</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Very small fire.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Your output transistor is touching the tin. It shorts every time it gets warm. And this capacitor is in backwards.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It works backwards.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It works until it explodes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Six kids look at her. She sighs and holds out her palm without looking at anyone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Iron.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rahul puts the soldering iron in her hand like a theatre nurse.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Twenty minutes on. The tea has gone cold. Anjali blows on a fresh joint.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Switch it on.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rahul turns the regulator. Across the shop the receiver gives a clean, empty hum. Rahul leans to the tin&apos;s little microphone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hello.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>From the receiver, half a second later, his own voice: &quot;Hello.&quot; The kids shriek.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>For a moment Anjali looks almost happy. Then she catches herself.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(low)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She used to let us do that. In the station. On Sundays.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I remember.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Do you?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali drinks the cold tea.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="10" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. THOMAS HOUSE - APPACHAN&apos;S ROOM - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A narrow room: a cot, a crucifix, a framed photograph of Mariamma at the station microphone, laughing at someone out of frame. By the door, a nail where the coir string of keys hangs at night. Tonight the keys are round Appachan&apos;s neck. Kannan sleeps on the floor.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali sits on a stool by the cot. Appachan&apos;s eyes open. They are clear. He knows her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(in Malayalam)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>മോളേ, ഇങ്ങു വാ. ഇരിക്ക്.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="General"><Text>[Note: SUBTITLE: Come here, child. Sit.]</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;m here, Appachan. It&apos;s Anjali.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He lifts the coir string over his head with shaking hands and holds out the keys.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>ഈ താക്കോൽ മറിയാമ്മയുടേതാണ്. ഇരുപത് കൊല്ലം ഞാനിത് കഴുത്തിലിട്ട് നടന്നു.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="General"><Text>[Note: SUBTITLE: These keys were Mariamma&apos;s. Twenty years I have carried them round my neck.]</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(in Malayalam)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>എനിക്ക് വേണ്ട, അപ്പച്ചാ.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="General"><Text>[Note: SUBTITLE: I don&apos;t want them, Appachan.]</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>അവരാ മുറി മണ്ണിട്ടു മൂടും. നീയത് സമ്മതിക്കരുത്.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="General"><Text>[Note: SUBTITLE: They will bury that room. Don&apos;t you let them.]</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Which room?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He frowns, as if she ought to know. He presses the keys into her palm and folds her fingers over them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>അവളന്ന് രാത്രി നിർത്തിയില്ല. എനിക്കറിയാം. ആരും വിശ്വസിച്ചില്ല.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="General"><Text>[Note: SUBTITLE: She did not stop that night. I know it. Nobody believed me.]</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>How do you know?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(looking at the photograph)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>എന്റെ റേഡിയോയിൽ ഞാൻ കേട്ടു. പന്ത്രണ്ടു മണിയാകും വരെ.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="General"><Text>[Note: SUBTITLE: I heard her on my radio. Until it was nearly midnight.]</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali looks at the watch on her wrist. 23:52.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>ഞാൻ നോക്കാം.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="General"><Text>[Note: SUBTITLE: I&apos;ll look.]</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>His eyes are already going. His hand finds her wrist, the one with the watch, and holds it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>നിനക്ക് അവളുടെ കൈയാണ്.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="General"><Text>[Note: SUBTITLE: You have her hands.]</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He sleeps. She does not move her wrist. On the floor, Kannan sighs.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="11" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. COAST RADIO STATION - OPERATIONS ROOM - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Dark. A key scrapes in a lock. A steel door shoves inward over grit. Two torch beams: Anjali, the keys wound round her fist, and Rahul in a head torch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The room from the prologue, twenty-three years on. The transmitter racks gutted, doors hanging. The desk. The wall chart rotted to a brown map of stains. Glass on the floor. Black mould climbing the walls like ferns. A bat goes past their heads.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(whispering)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Why are we whispering?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(whispering)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Because it&apos;s a crime.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Her torch moves along the tape shelves on the back wall. Steel shelves, stencilled by month. Every one empty. Clean outlines in the dust where boxes stood.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They cleared it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Regional office. Form 14. Fathima aunty told the whole village about Form 14.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali goes to the desk and puts her hand flat where the microphone stood. She kneels and looks underneath. The space where a girl could sleep. A fertiliser sack rotted to lace. She stays there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rahul has opened a side panel of the big transmitter cabinet with a screwdriver, more from habit than hope.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Chechi.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Wedged behind the dead valves, a square biscuit box, warped and stained. Rahul works it out and lifts the lid.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A reel of quarter-inch tape on a plastic hub. The tape has swollen to a dull brown, bloomed white with salt at the edges.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>CLOSE ON THE REEL LABEL: 14-11-1999.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Round, quick handwriting. Under the date, smaller: KDK ops, log tape, M.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&apos;s the night.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Don&apos;t touch the tape. Close the box.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He closes it. She takes it from him with both hands.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(barely)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She hid it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>From who?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Outside, a whistle. Sharp and close.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="12" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. COAST RADIO STATION - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A GUARD (60s), a retired soldier in a security company shirt, swings a long flashlight along the fence and blows his whistle again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>GUARD</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who&apos;s there? I have a stick!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali and Rahul come out of the station door low and fast. She pushes the biscuit box inside her shirt and holds it there with one arm.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>GUARD (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Stop! The police are coming!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(running)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No they&apos;re not!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>GUARD</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They&apos;re coming tomorrow!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Through the gap in the fence and into the coconut grove. Moonlight in stripes between the trunks. Fronds crack underfoot. The flashlight beam swings across the palms behind them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rahul&apos;s bicycle leans against a palm. He jumps on and pedals twice. The chain comes off with a clank.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No, no, no...</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Push it!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They run with the bicycle between them, chain dragging, bell ringing every time the wheel hits a root. Behind them the guard gives up. One last whistle, almost sad.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>On the road they stop, bent double, breathing hard. Rahul starts laughing. Anjali tries not to and can&apos;t.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>&quot;They&apos;re coming tomorrow.&quot;</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He&apos;ll tell Suresh.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Let him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She takes the box out of her shirt. Still closed. Still dry. She carries it against her chest all the way home, the loose chain ticking on the road beside her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="13" StartsNewPage="Yes" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. FISHERIES SCHOOL - RADIO CLUB ROOM - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A classroom nobody else wanted: a bucket under a leak, a blackboard with a half-erased drawing of fish gills, a cupboard of donated electronics, a map of the Arabian Sea edged with postcards from distant radio stations.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Under an anglepoise lamp the reel lies on clean white paper. Anjali, in cotton gloves, lifts one edge of the outer layer with a wooden toothpick. Rahul, Meenu and four other kids lean in. Fathima stands behind them with a tiffin carrier.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>CLOSE ON THE OXIDE AS IT LIFTS FROM THE BACKING IN DULL BROWN FLAKES, LIKE SCALES FROM A FISH.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MEENU</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It looks like fish.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&apos;s called sticky-shed. The glue that holds the oxide on drinks water. Twenty years by the sea, and this has drunk the sea.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>So we can&apos;t play it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Like this, it sticks to the heads and tears. One pass, then nothing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>So what do we do?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We bake it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MEENU</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Like a cake?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Like a cake nobody eats. Fifty-four degrees for eight hours, then it cools for a day. That drives the water out for a while. Then we play it once, maybe twice, and record everything, because we won&apos;t get it again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And I need a quarter-inch machine. A good one.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The church has a Nagra. Father bought it for the Bishop&apos;s visit in ninety-six. It&apos;s in the sacristy in a suitcase.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(almost a laugh)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A Nagra. That&apos;s the machine she recorded on.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And the oven?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It has to hold fifty-four for eight hours and not move. A kitchen oven swings by twenty. It would cook it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(already on her feet)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Babu at the bakery has a convection oven from Dubai. He&apos;ll lend it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Will he?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He owes me eleven thousand rupees in tea.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="14" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. FATHIMA&apos;S TEA SHOP - KITCHEN - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The back kitchen, hot and narrow, blackened pans on hooks. Two men from the bakery wheel in a stainless steel convection oven and lift it onto the table. The factory plastic is still on its panel.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rahul has built an alarm: a thermometer probe pushed through the door seal, wired to a buzzer and a nine-volt battery in a soap dish.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Over fifty-seven, this screams. Under fifty, it also screams. A different scream.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>How different?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You&apos;ll know.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali lays the reel flat on a wire rack, hub down, on a paper-lined tray, and slides it in like a surgeon. She closes the door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>INSERT: THE OVEN DISPLAY. 54.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Eight hours. Nobody opens the door. Nobody puts samosas in it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MEENU</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(insulted)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nobody was going to.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(who was)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nobody.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They stand and look at the oven. Its fan hums. Nothing happens. Nothing goes on happening.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MEENU</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>How long now?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Four minutes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fathima sets a plastic chair facing the oven and sits. Rahul brings another. Meenu sits on the floor. Anjali stays standing, arms folded.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>My mother sat like this when my brother was in the incubator.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Did he make it?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He lives in Sharjah and never calls his mother. So yes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The alarm gives one small chirp. Everybody jumps. The display still reads 54.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Loose wire.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Fix it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="15" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. THOMAS HOUSE - VERANDA - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Late afternoon. A tiled veranda, a low wall, a cane chair, red chillies drying on a string. Beyond the palms the sea flashes in pieces.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali comes up the steps. Appachan sits in the cane chair in his good white shirt, buttoned wrong, his hair combed flat with water. Kannan at his feet. He sees her and his face sets hard.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>മറിയാമ്മേ. What time is it? The shift starts at six. You think the sea waits because you&apos;re at the market?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Appachan, I&apos;m...</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She stops. She looks at the wrong buttons, at how ready he is.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Varghese is going out tonight. Eleven boats. You&apos;ll be talking to them all night and you haven&apos;t eaten.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She sits on the low wall, facing him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(gently)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The sea is calm tonight, Appachan. Wind off the land. Swell under a metre. Nobody needs me tonight.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Calm?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Calm. They&apos;ll all be home for breakfast.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Something lets go in his shoulders. He sits back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Good. Then sit with me, മോളേ.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She takes the coir string of keys from her bag and loops it back over his head. He touches the keys, satisfied. Then she undoes his shirt and buttons it again properly, from the top. He lets her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="16" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. FISHERIES SCHOOL - RADIO CLUB ROOM - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Blinds down. One lamp. The Nagra sits open on the desk, a silver machine with a big meter needle. Anjali has threaded the baked reel through the heads by hand. A cable runs to a laptop, a pair of studio headphones and a single speaker.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The club sits on the desks and the floor. Fathima stands by the door. Even the ceiling fan is off.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="General"><Text>[Note: Note to the sound department: the reel playback in this scene is recorded live on set from the Nagra through the practical speaker. Mariamma&apos;s lines are recorded in prep and aged to match the reel. Do not replace the room sound in post.]</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Once I press this, nobody moves. If I say stop, I&apos;ve already stopped it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What if it&apos;s nothing?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then it&apos;s nothing, and we&apos;ll know.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She arms the recording on the laptop. Her finger on the play lever is steady. Her other hand is not. She pushes the lever.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>CLOSE ON THE NAGRA&apos;S REELS TURNING. THE TAPE SLIDES OVER THE HEADS AND A LITTLE BROWN DUST LIFTS FROM IT.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hiss. The room tone of a room that no longer exists. A clock. Rain.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then, close and warm in the speaker, a woman&apos;s laugh.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Meenu grabs Rahul&apos;s sleeve.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(on the tape)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No, Joe, I&apos;m not going to say it like that, they&apos;ll think somebody died...</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Static swallows her. The meter needle swings and drops. Then, clear:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (V.O.) (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Little Flower, Little Flower, this is Kadalkkara. Little Flower, this is Kadalkkara, do you...</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A sound like paper tearing. On the left reel, layers of tape lift away together in one stuck block.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali snaps the lever back. Silence. Nobody moves.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(very quietly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The second half is stuck to itself. If I pull it, it tears.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She puts both hands flat on the table either side of the Nagra. She is shaking now, all of her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fathima comes up behind her and just stands there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That was her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(nodding)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That was her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MEENU</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(whispering)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She was funny.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She was very funny.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(she stares at the reel)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It has to go back in the oven. Longer. I don&apos;t know if the second half survives it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>So we bake it again, Anju.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="17" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. JOE&apos;S ROOM ABOVE THE TODDY SHOP - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A rented room up an outside stair, above the toddy shop&apos;s tin roof. Empty arrack bottles stand along one wall like a fence. A cot made with military neatness. One framed photograph on the wall, turned to face the plaster.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>JOE (60), grey, heavy in the face, in a very clean shirt, pours tea into a single glass and gives it to Anjali. He pours nothing for himself.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You look like her round the mouth. Not the eyes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I want to ask you about the fourteenth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everybody has asked me about the fourteenth. The Port. The police. A newspaper from Kottayam. I said what I said. It&apos;s written down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;ve read it. The main transmitter failed in the storm at 23:40 and you couldn&apos;t get it back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&apos;s right.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We found a reel.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>His hand is on the kettle. It moves, very slightly. The lid rattles.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>In the transmitter cabinet. Her handwriting. The fourteenth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(without turning round)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The station tapes went to the regional office in 2004.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Not this one.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He sets the kettle down. His hand is shaking. He puts it in his pocket.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;d like you to go now. I&apos;m asking politely, Anjali.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the door she looks at the photograph turned to the wall.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is that the station?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Go home.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She goes. He stands in the middle of the room a long time. Then he kneels and takes a bottle from under the cot.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="18" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. ST. ANTONY&apos;S CHURCH - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A seafront church, its white paint eaten by salt. Marigold garlands on the steps. A cloth banner between two palms: 14 November 1999, We Remember.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Two hundred villagers in white, the men in mundus, the women in white saris with black borders. Under a canvas awning a CHOIR of twelve sings a slow hymn to a harmonium.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali stands at the very back by the cycle stand, in a borrowed white churidar. Fathima beside her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>On the church steps Suresh, in white, unfolds a sheet of paper. The choir finishes. There is only the sea.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Varghese Pillai.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hamza Kutty.
Sebastian Fernandez.
Thankachan Joseph.
Kunjumon Antony.
Paulose Mathew.
Antony Xavier.
Babu Rajan.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At Hamza&apos;s name, Fathima&apos;s lips move with his.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Eight men. Three boats. Little Flower, Seagull, Morning Star.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(he folds the paper)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We know what happened to them. We have always known.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Heads turn toward the back. One, then three, then twenty. Toward Anjali.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Suresh does not look at her. He looks at the sea.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fathima&apos;s hand finds Anjali&apos;s and holds on.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(under her breath)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Don&apos;t go.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;m not going anywhere.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="19" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. POLICE OUTPOST - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>One front room with a counter, a wireless set muttering, faded posters. At the back a single cell, its door open, a constable&apos;s lunch box on the bunk.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Latha, in reading glasses, turns a typed complaint round so Anjali can read it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Trespass on Port Trust property. Damage to a fence. Theft of one item, unspecified. Your name. Rahul&apos;s name, spelled wrong.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The fence was already cut. My grandfather cut it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>With what?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>His teeth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Latha almost smiles. She does not.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The contractor sends me a letter, I answer the letter. I have to ask you about an unspecified item.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I don&apos;t have an unspecified item.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Good. Keep not having it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She gets up, shuts the front door, turns the wireless down. She opens the bottom drawer of her desk with a key from her own key ring and takes out a thin cardboard file, its string tie gone grey. She puts it on the desk. She does not push it across.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>INSERT: THE FILE COVER. MISSING PERSON. MARIAMMA THOMAS, 38. KADALKKARA. 15-11-1999.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Why is it in your drawer?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Because nobody ever closed it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You read it tonight and it&apos;s on my desk at nine tomorrow, or I arrest you for the fence.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali takes the file and holds it on her knees with both hands, the way she held the reel.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Thank you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(glasses back on)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I didn&apos;t give you anything. Go away.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>CUT TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="20" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. THOMAS HOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>One bulb. The file open on the table: a typed statement, a sketch map of the station and the jetty, a carbon copy of a Port Trust incident report. Anjali and Fathima sit side by side.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(reading)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>&quot;The transmitter ceased operation at approximately 23:40 owing to storm damage to the main supply. Station chief J. D&apos;Cruz left the premises at approximately 23:50 to seek assistance.&quot;</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>To seek assistance.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>&quot;At approximately 00:05 Operator M. Thomas was seen by the ice plant watchman walking toward the north jetty carrying a lantern.&quot;</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(she turns the page)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&apos;s the last thing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everybody says what she was doing. She went down to the boats because the station went quiet and it was her fault and she couldn&apos;t live with it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is that what you say?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fathima takes off her glasses and lays them on the file.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>My uncle Hamza was on the Seagull.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You never said.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Not once. Not to you, when you cried in my bed every night for a month. I thought if I said Seagull, you&apos;d hear blame.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He taught me to swim. He threw me off the jetty and counted. I came up at seven every time and he&apos;d say, slow, Fathima, the fish are laughing at you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;m sorry.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;m not telling you so you&apos;ll be sorry.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(she taps the time on the page: 23:40)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;ve carried this village&apos;s tea for twenty-three years. I want to know too.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali puts her hand over Fathima&apos;s on the file.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Okay.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="21" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. KADALKKARA BEACH - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A bright, windy morning. The radio club&apos;s kite, a square of old blue fishing net on bamboo with a tail of plastic bags, climbs and holds. A long copper wire runs down the kite line to a stake in the sand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the stake, on a plastic chair, sits the club&apos;s battered marine transceiver, borrowed off a boat. Anjali kneels beside it. Fathima watches from under a beach umbrella with a flask.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You don&apos;t say hello. You say their name, then yours, then you stop and let them answer.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What&apos;s our name?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You haven&apos;t got one yet. Say radio club.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She hands him the handset. He holds it as if it were hot.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(keying)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kadalkkara Harbour, Kadalkkara Harbour, this is the radio club. Over.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Static. Wind. Nothing. Meenu&apos;s face falls.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Wait.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A gull goes over. Then, flat and bored:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HARBOUR CLERK (ON RADIO)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Radio club, Harbour. What do you want? Over.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>One second of silence. Then Rahul and Meenu both start yelling at once.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He answered! Harbour, this is us! We&apos;re on the beach! We made a kite!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MEENU</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He answered! Rahul, he answered! Tell him about the kite!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The smaller kids scream and run in circles.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HARBOUR CLERK (ON RADIO)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Radio club, take your thumb off the key. Over.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali is laughing. Not politely. Bent over, one hand flat on the sand, laughing the way her mother laughed on the cassette.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Under the umbrella, Fathima sees it and stops pouring.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(wiping her eyes)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Say thank you and say out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(keying, very formal)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Thank you, Harbour. Radio club, out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HARBOUR CLERK (ON RADIO)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Go to school. Harbour, out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="22" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>OMITTED</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="23" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. KADALKKARA HARBOUR - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text RevisionID="1">Anjali runs out of the lane with a torch, shouting for Appachan. Behind her the front door of the house bangs open and shut. The cot inside is empty; Kannan&apos;s lead lies on the floor.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rain in sheets. Waves burst on the harbour wall and come down on the road like thrown buckets.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fishermen in plastic sheets with torches and lanterns shout names into the wind. Kids on bicycles ride the road along the wall, ringing their bells.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(on his bicycle)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Appachan! Kannan!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fathima, a sari over her head, shines a torch into wheelhouses.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Raghavan chetta, the net sheds!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali comes running, soaked through.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The station?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Latha&apos;s there. He isn&apos;t. Not the church, not the bus stand, not the ice plant. Anju, he isn&apos;t anywhere.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Headlights. A contractor&apos;s flatbed site truck, a generator and two folded floodlight towers on the back, stops with its wipers going. Suresh jumps down in a rain jacket with two of his CREW.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to his crew)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Towers up. One on the wharf, one at the root of the breakwater.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The generator coughs and roars. The lights come up hard and white, and all at once the harbour has edges.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to the fishermen)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Mani, take five men north to the river. Joy, go south. Nobody goes on the wall without a rope.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nobody argues. They go. Suresh sees Anjali, rain pouring off them both.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>When did you last see him?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nine. Asleep. The dog was with him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Suresh looks past the lights at the long black arm of the breakwater. Its far end is under white water.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He used to stand out there when we were kids. With a lantern, waiting for the boats.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That was twenty years ago.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Not for him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He is already walking.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="23A" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. KADALKKARA JETTY - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text RevisionID="1"></Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text RevisionID="1">The search reaches the jetty. Anjali stops at the last plank, where the police file says her mother was last seen. Rain on the water. She shines her torch down into the dark and does not move.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text RevisionID="1">FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text RevisionID="1">(gently)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text RevisionID="1">He is not here. Come. Suresh has the lights on the breakwater.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text RevisionID="1">Anjali turns away from the water and runs.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="24" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. HARBOUR BREAKWATER - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>WIDE ON THE BREAKWATER: A LONG SPINE OF CONCRETE TETRAPODS RUNNING INTO THE STORM, FLOODLIT AT THE ROOT, BLACK AT THE FAR END. EVERY FEW SECONDS A WAVE GOES OVER IT WHOLE.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="General"><Text>[Note: Note to stunts and safety: on the far tetrapods Suresh and Appachan are stunt doubles on the wave rig, harnessed and cabled to the hidden rail. The principals work only on the dressed section at the root.]</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Suresh climbs onto the first tetrapod with a coil of rope. Anjali scrambles up behind him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Stay here and hold the rope.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He&apos;s my grandfather.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He was my father&apos;s friend. Hold the rope.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He throws her the end and goes out, crouching, three points of contact. A wave comes. He drops flat and holds on. It goes over him. He is still there. He goes on.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>On the last block stands Appachan, in his vest and mundu, soaked through, swinging a torch in slow arcs out to sea. Kannan is pressed against his legs, barking at the water.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(shouting at the sea)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Here! This way! Varghese! The river mouth!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Suresh reaches him. Appachan fights him, surprisingly strong.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Let go! They can&apos;t see the light!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Appachan, it&apos;s Suresh. Varghese&apos;s boy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Appachan stops. Water streams down both their faces.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(confused)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Your father&apos;s out there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A long moment.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I know. He&apos;s coming in. He saw your light. They all saw it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He saw it?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He saw it. Come home now.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Appachan&apos;s legs give. Suresh wraps him in his rain jacket and lifts him. An old man weighing nothing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Behind them a wave rises, the biggest yet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(at the root)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Suresh!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It breaks. White over everything. The torch is torn from Appachan&apos;s hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>UNDERWATER: THE TORCH TUMBLES DOWN THROUGH GREEN-BLACK WATER, ITS BEAM TURNING OVER AND OVER, FADING.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The water drains off the blocks. Suresh is still there, on his knees, Appachan in his arms. Kannan scrambles up out of a gap and shakes himself.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Suresh walks back, block by block. Anjali hauls in the rope hand over hand as he comes, as if she were pulling them both in. She takes Appachan&apos;s face in her hands.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(eyes closed, teeth chattering)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He saw the light.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hospital. Now.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="25" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. TALUK HOSPITAL - WARD - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A long men&apos;s ward. Twenty iron beds, ceiling fans, half of them turning. Somebody coughing. Somebody&apos;s radio playing old film songs very low.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Appachan sleeps under two hospital blankets, a drip in the back of his hand, oxygen prongs in his nose.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A NURSE (40s) checks the drip and writes on the chart.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>NURSE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>His temperature is coming up. Old people get cold and forget how to get warm.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(she hands Anjali a plastic bag)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>His things.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A wet vest, a mundu, and the coir string of keys. Anjali takes out the keys and holds them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>NURSE (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Only one person stays the night.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;m staying.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The nurse looks at Suresh, soaked, sitting on the next bed in a puddle.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>NURSE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Bed twelve is for a real patient. Get off it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Suresh gets up and stands against the wall. The nurse moves on.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Thank you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Don&apos;t.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(he looks at Appachan)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He used to take me out on his boat on Sundays, when my father was drinking. Told me I had soft hands and I&apos;d better study.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You got the ice candy job.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He almost laughs. It doesn&apos;t come out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>My father&apos;s boat had the best set in the fleet. A Japanese set. He paid it off over three years.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(he looks at his hands)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That night he could hear Kollam. He could hear a ship off Colombo. And he couldn&apos;t hear Kadalkkara. Because your mother had gone off the air.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You don&apos;t know that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Every skipper who came home said it. The station went quiet at twenty to twelve. They followed my father&apos;s lantern to the river mouth. Then he went back out for the Seagull, alone, because nobody was on the radio telling anybody anything.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And you blame her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I was on the beach with a torch, like an idiot. Like him tonight. I blamed God for a year. Then the sea. Then I got tired and blamed the person who was supposed to be talking.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&apos;s easier than it sounds.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali unbuckles the watch and holds it out. He does not take it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It was hers. It stopped at eight minutes to twelve. She put it on my wrist that night and told me to wind it at midnight. I fell asleep.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(she buckles it back on)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She didn&apos;t stop at twenty to twelve, Suresh. There&apos;s a reel.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The thing you stole.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The thing she hid.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Appachan stirs and sleeps again. They both watch him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="26" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. TALUK HOSPITAL - CORRIDOR - DAWN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Grey light at the end of a long corridor. A vending machine hums. Relatives asleep on benches and on newspapers on the floor.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Suresh gets two paper cups of tea from the machine and hands one to Anjali. His shirt has dried on him in creases.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What&apos;s on the reel?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A laugh. Half a sentence. &quot;Little Flower, this is Kadalkkara.&quot; The rest is stuck. It&apos;s baking again now, in a bakery oven, with a sixteen-year-old&apos;s alarm on it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&apos;s your evidence.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;m not making a case. I want to hear the end of it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The excavator comes the day after tomorrow. It&apos;s booked. It&apos;s paid for. The Port wants the land clear, and so do I.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;ll look away for one day. Today, until six. My guard takes a long lunch. If there&apos;s nothing in there, you let it go. You let him let it go. You let me let it go.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And if there&apos;s something?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then I&apos;ll be standing next to you when you find it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She holds out her cup. He taps his against it. Neither of them drinks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="27" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. COAST RADIO STATION - TAPE ROOM - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A basement under the operations room, down a concrete stair. Black water stands knee-deep. Daylight falls through a single grille in one slanting bar. Swollen tape boxes float everywhere, open and empty.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali wades in wearing rubber boots, a torch in her hand. Rahul follows in shorts and a head torch. Suresh stays at the top of the stairs in his site boots, a hard hat in his hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Mind the wiring. There&apos;s no power, but mind it anyway.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali pushes a floating box aside. Empty. Another. Empty.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What are we looking for?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Appachan said they&apos;d bury the room.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(she looks up at the ceiling)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I thought he meant upstairs.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>ANGLE ON THE UNDERSIDE OF THE CONCRETE STAIRS, BLACK AND DRIPPING. SOMETHING SQUARE AND OLIVE GREEN IS BOLTED UP UNDER THE TREADS.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rahul crouches until the water reaches his waist and ducks under the stairs.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Chechi. There&apos;s a box.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>An army surplus ammunition box, bolted under the stairs with two rusted brackets. A padlock.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(coming down three steps)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&apos;s a fuse box.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&apos;s an ammunition box. My uncle keeps his fish hooks in one.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali pulls the coir string of keys from under her shirt. One key. Another. The third is small and brass, newer than the rest. It turns.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Together she and Rahul ease the box off its brackets. She holds it up clear of the water.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Suresh comes the rest of the way down into the water without seeming to notice he has done it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She sets the box on a step and lifts the lid. A rubber seal sighs. Inside, dry, wrapped in a plastic rice sack, a ledger bound in green cloth. Stencilled on the cover: KDK Coast Radio. Station Log. 1999.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nobody speaks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="28" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. COAST RADIO STATION - OPERATIONS ROOM - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Daylight through the broken shutters across the old desk. Anjali sits where her mother sat, the logbook open. Rahul perches on the desk. Suresh stands at the window, not quite in the room.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The pages are ruled in columns: time, station, traffic, operator. A different hand for each shift. Anjali turns to the fourteenth of November and runs her finger down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(reading)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Eighteen hundred. Mariamma on watch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="LOGBOOK"><Text>18:00 M. THOMAS ON WATCH. WEATHER TO ALL STATIONS. WIND W 20 KNOTS AND RISING.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="LOGBOOK"><Text>21:30 ELEVEN BOATS AT SEA, ALL REPORTING: LITTLE FLOWER, SEAGULL, MORNING STAR, BISMILLAH, SEA QUEEN, AMBADI, JYOTHI, MOTHER MARY, ST. ANTONY, KAIRALI, POURNAMI.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="LOGBOOK"><Text>22:50 CYCLONE WARNING RELAYED FROM DISTRICT. ALL BOATS ADVISED TO RETURN.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="LOGBOOK"><Text>23:15 LITTLE FLOWER AND SEAGULL REPORT HEAVY SWELL, 9 MILES WSW.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="LOGBOOK"><Text>23:32 SEAGULL NOT ANSWERING. LITTLE FLOWER STANDING BY.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Twenty-three thirty-two. Seagull not answering.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Her finger stops. The next entry is in another hand, heavier, pressed hard into the paper.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="LOGBOOK"><Text>23:40 MAIN POWER CUT BY ORDER. J. D&apos;CRUZ.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Suresh crosses the room in three steps and reads it over her shoulder.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>By order.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It doesn&apos;t say storm damage.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Below Joe&apos;s line, in the round quick hand from the reel label, a single small entry.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="LOGBOOK"><Text>23:41 ON BATTERY.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(almost a whisper)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>On battery.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What does that mean?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It means somebody switched her off, and she didn&apos;t stay off.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Suresh puts his hard hat down on the desk as if it were made of glass. He looks at the doorway.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Joe D&apos;Cruz said it was the storm. He said it to my mother in our front room. He held her hand when he said it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali closes the logbook and stands.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Where does he drink now?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Same place he always did.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>CUT TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="29" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. TODDY SHOP - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A palm-thatched toddy shop at the edge of the backwater. A yard of packed earth under a mango tree, long tables, a string of bare bulbs, clay pots of toddy, plates of tapioca and fish curry. Twenty DRINKERS, fishermen mostly. A cassette player on the counter plays an old film song.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Joe sits alone at the end of a table with a glass and a plate he has not touched.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali walks into the yard with the logbook against her chest. Suresh stays at the gate.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Heads turn. Women do not come in here. The talk dies table by table. Somebody turns the cassette player off.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Joe.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Not here.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You told me it&apos;s written down. You were right.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She opens the logbook on the table in front of him, on top of his tapioca, and says it from memory, loud enough for the yard.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>&quot;Twenty-three forty. Main power cut by order. J. D&apos;Cruz.&quot;</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&apos;s your hand. Whose order, Joe?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The yard is silent. A frog in the backwater. The bulbs buzz.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Joe stands, unsteady. He empties every note from his wallet onto the counter.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to the whole yard)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everybody&apos;s drinks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nobody moves.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the gate Suresh does not step aside. Joe has to turn sideways to get past him. Neither speaks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Joe goes out to an old cream Ambassador parked under a streetlight. He tries the key twice. The engine catches.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to herself)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He&apos;s drunk.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The Ambassador pulls out, its one working headlight sweeping across the yard and away.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="30" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. LIGHTHOUSE ROAD - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A narrow coast road on a ridge between the sea and the backwater. The lighthouse beam sweeps over it every twelve seconds.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The Ambassador weaves onto the gravel shoulder, corrects too hard, drifts across the centre line. A cow stands in the road in the headlight, chewing, a bell round its neck. The car swerves round it with a long horn and a spray of gravel. The cow does not move.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Ahead, the road bends sharply over a drop to the rocks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A blue light behind. Latha&apos;s jeep overtakes on the outside, cuts in and stops across the road. The Ambassador slides on the wet gravel and stops a metre short of the jeep&apos;s door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Latha walks to the driver&apos;s window. Joe sits with both hands on the wheel, looking straight ahead. She reaches in, switches off the engine, takes the keys out of the ignition and puts them in her pocket. Neither of them says a word.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She opens his door and waits. After a long moment he gets out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(finally)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Third time.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I know.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I never wrote up the first two.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I know.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The lighthouse beam goes over them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Get in the jeep.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="31" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. POLICE OUTPOST - CELL - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The single cell. Its door stands open. It has never been locked. Joe sits on the concrete bunk under a bare bulb with a steel tumbler of black coffee.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Latha sits on a plastic chair in the doorway of the cell. Anjali stands in the outer room by the counter, the logbook under her arm. She has come no closer.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Joe. She&apos;s here.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to his coffee)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>At twenty past eleven a telegram came from the Port office. A boy rode a bicycle through that rain.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I saw you. In the doorway.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(looking up for the first time)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You were asleep.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I wasn&apos;t.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He takes that in.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text RevisionID="1">Shut down the main transmitter at 23:40. Lightning risk. The Port had just put up a new signal tower in the same ground as our mast.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It said it was an order. It had a number on it. I had a loan on the house. I pulled the breaker myself and I wrote it in the book. I thought, one day somebody will read this and know it was them, not me.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then I tore the telegram up and told everyone it was the storm. Because nobody reads the book.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What did she do?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There was an emergency set. An old army battery set, two car batteries, on the emergency aerial on the roof. Half the range. Less.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She wired it up in the dark by a candle. I told her she&apos;d be sacked. I told her she&apos;d be killed if lightning hit the mast. I said, Anjali is here. Think of Anjali.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali&apos;s hand tightens on the logbook.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She gave me the book and told me to write whatever I liked. Then she put the headphones on and started calling Varghese.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And you?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I went home.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I sat in my kitchen with the radio on and couldn&apos;t hear her. The set was too weak to reach the village. I thought she was sitting in the dark talking to nobody.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She wasn&apos;t talking to nobody.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(very quietly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I went back at one. The candle was out. The set was cold. The lantern was gone off its hook. And she was gone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A long silence. The bulb buzzes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You let them say it was her. For twenty-three years.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Yes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He reaches out and holds the bars of the open cell door. Not to keep himself in. Just to hold on to something.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>CLOSE ON JOE&apos;S HANDS ON THE BARS, KNUCKLES WHITE.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>MATCH CUT TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="32" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. COAST RADIO STATION - OPERATIONS ROOM - NIGHT (1999)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Younger hands, gripping a doorframe. Joe, 1999, soaked, in the doorway.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>One candle on the desk, stuck in its own wax on a saucer. The valve racks dead. Rain.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mariamma kneels beside an olive green emergency set, a heavy steel box with a handle, two car batteries next to it. She clips a lead to a battery by feel. A small blue crack of sparks. She does not flinch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Mariamma, leave it. It&apos;s an order from the Port.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The Port isn&apos;t out there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>If lightning comes down that mast, it comes down that aerial and into you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then hold the candle further away.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She clips the second lead. A green lamp on the set glows. A hum.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Under the desk, Young Anjali, wide awake, holds her transistor against her chest.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>POV YOUNG ANJALI: HER MOTHER&apos;S FACE IN THE CANDLELIGHT, LIT GREEN FROM BELOW BY THE LAMP ON THE SET.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Anjali is here. Think of Anjali. Come home.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I am thinking of Anjali.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She stands, takes the logbook off the desk and pushes it into his chest.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Here. Write whatever you like. Then go home, Joe. Somebody should be dry tonight.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He writes, standing, the book braced against the doorframe. He lays it on the desk and goes out into the rain without looking back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Mariamma reads his line. She writes one short line under it. She checks the Nagra. Its reels still turn on their own batteries.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She kneels by the desk. Young Anjali looks out at her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>YOUNG ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Are we in trouble?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Joe&apos;s in trouble. We&apos;re working.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She buckles the wristwatch onto her daughter&apos;s thin wrist, on the last hole. It is still too big.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You&apos;re the clock now. At midnight you wind it, and you tell me it&apos;s twelve.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>YOUNG ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What happens at twelve?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Low tide.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She puts on the headphones and takes up the handset of the battery set.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into the handset)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Little Flower, Little Flower, this is Kadalkkara. Varghese, you&apos;ll have to listen hard. I&apos;m very small tonight.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Under the desk, Young Anjali looks at the watch. Her eyes begin to close.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="33" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. FISHERIES SCHOOL - RADIO CLUB ROOM - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The lamp. The Nagra. The reel has been baked a second time, for twelve hours, and has cooled. The club sits on the floor. Fathima at the door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It came apart this afternoon. Layer by layer, with a toothpick.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Does it play?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We find out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She presses the lever. The reels turn.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(on the tape, small, through heavy noise)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Little Flower, Little Flower, this is Kadalkkara, on battery. Varghese, you&apos;ll have to listen hard. I&apos;m very small tonight.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A long crackle. Meenu puts her hand over her mouth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VARGHESE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(on the tape, distorted, far away)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kadalkkara, Little Flower. Weak, but I have you. Go.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali&apos;s hand moves to the fader and holds the level steady.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>River mouth. Nine fifty-eight north, seventy-six fifteen east. Bring them in on the church tower and the ice plant chimney in a line. Keep to the north side of the bar. Over.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VARGHESE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Little Flower copies. Church and chimney. North side. I&apos;ll take them in. I&apos;ll light the boats.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Light them, Varghese. Tell them it&apos;s you. They&apos;ll follow you. They never listen to me.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rahul is crying silently, his hands in his lap. Meenu leans against his shoulder. Anjali&apos;s eyes stay on the meter. Her hand stays on the fader. Steady.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (V.O.) (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Seagull, Seagull, Kadalkkara. Hamza, give me a light.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the door, Fathima puts her hand flat against the wall.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VARGHESE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kadalkkara, Little Flower. Eight boats inside the bar. I&apos;m going back for Hamza. Mariamma, I&apos;m going back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Varghese, don&apos;t...</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(a long pause)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Go carefully. I&apos;ll be here.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Her voice is thinning now as the battery drops, as if she were walking away down a corridor.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (V.O.) (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kadalkkara. Battery&apos;s going. Time is twenty-three fifty-one. Anjali&apos;s asleep.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(very faint)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;ll put the lantern on the jetty for you, Varghese. Look for the lantern.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hiss. Only hiss. The meter needle lies down flat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali looks at the watch on her wrist. 23:52.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She lets the reel run out. The tail of the tape flaps round on the take-up spool. Tick. Tick. Tick.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(barely)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She said Hamza.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She said everybody&apos;s name.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>DISSOLVE TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="34" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. OPEN SEA - LITTLE FLOWER - NIGHT (1999)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The wheelhouse. Varghese has the handset pressed to his ear, one arm round the wheel. With the pencil stub he writes on the back of his hand: 958 7615.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VARGHESE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into the handset)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nine fifty-eight, seventy-six fifteen. Church and chimney.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (ON RADIO)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(tiny, far)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&apos;s it. North side.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He takes a lit hurricane lantern off its hook and goes out on deck into the rain.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>POV VARGHESE: MASTHEAD LIGHTS SCATTERED OVER THE SWELL, SOME NEAR, SOME FAR, ALL OF THEM LOST.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He climbs onto the wheelhouse roof, one hand on the mast, and swings the lantern in a slow arc over his head. The fishermen&apos;s signal. Follow me.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>One light turns. Then another. Then five. The boats come round and fall in behind the Little Flower in a ragged line.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>His deckhand shouts and points. Far off to the west, a hull rides low with no lights, side-on to the sea. The Seagull. Drifting.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Varghese looks at the line of boats behind him, nearly home. Then at the Seagull. He drops back into the wheelhouse and takes the wheel.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VARGHESE (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to the deckhand)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hold on to something.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He turns the Little Flower away from the river mouth, back into the dark.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The handset swings on its cord.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (ON RADIO)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(barely there)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Look for the lantern.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>CUT TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="35" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. SURESH&apos;S HOUSE - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A small, very neat concrete house. A polished red floor. Plastic flowers. On the wall, in a gilt frame, a photograph of the Little Flower on her launch day, men crowded on her deck with garlands. In the middle of them a younger Varghese, laughing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali sets a small digital recorder and a pair of closed headphones on the dining table. Suresh stands across the table from her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&apos;s eleven minutes. His voice comes in at four. I took a little of the hiss down. I didn&apos;t cut anything.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Why not?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It isn&apos;t mine to cut.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She goes out. The door closes behind her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Suresh stands for a while. Then he sits, puts on the headphones and presses play.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Very faint, leaking from the earcups:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VARGHESE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(through the headphones)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Little Flower copies. I&apos;ll take them in. I&apos;ll light the boats.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>POV ANJALI, THROUGH THE WINDOW FROM THE FRONT STEP: SURESH AT THE TABLE IN THE HEADPHONES, VERY STILL. SLOWLY HE PUTS ONE HAND FLAT ON THE TABLE AND LEAVES IT THERE.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>BACK TO SCENE.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The recording ends. Suresh takes the headphones off and lays them down very gently. He goes to the photograph and straightens it, though it was already straight.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He opens the front door. Anjali sits on the step with her back to him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He sounds young.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He was younger than you are now.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(a beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He said, I&apos;m going back. He knew.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They both knew.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Come inside. I&apos;ll make tea. I make terrible tea.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She gets up and goes in.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="36" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. PORT TRUST OFFICE - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The same fans. Krishnadas at his desk. Suresh sits in the chair where Anjali sat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PORT OFFICER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Mr. Pillai, your own company booked the excavator. The container yard contractor starts piling on the twentieth. If I move your date, I am explaining to Ernakulam why.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>One week.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PORT OFFICER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Not one day.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There is a station log that says the Port ordered that transmitter shut down at 23:40 on the fourteenth of November 1999. In a cyclone. With eleven boats out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Krishnadas stops writing and looks up properly for the first time.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PORT OFFICER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(carefully)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>If there is such a document, there will be an inquiry. Inquiries take nine years. The building comes down tomorrow either way.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I understand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(he slides a typed sheet across)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then sign this. The site generator, the truck and two of my men, tonight, on the beach below the station. One night.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PORT OFFICER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>For what?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A listening.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Krishnadas signs the sheet without reading it and stamps it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PORT OFFICER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(handing it back)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>My father was on the Jyothi. He came home.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="37" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. KADALKKARA BEACH - DUSK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The sun going down red into the sea. Fishing boats pulled up in a long row. Kids on bamboo ladders string paper lanterns and kerosene lanterns between the masts, a line of small lights the length of the beach.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fathima at a trestle table with her copper urn and a stack of steel tumblers. Rahul and Meenu run cable from the Nagra to two big loudspeakers on bamboo stands, borrowed from the church festival.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The contractor&apos;s generator truck grinds onto the sand, two of Suresh&apos;s crew pushing at the tailboard. Suresh walks backward in front of it, guiding it in.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Left. Stop. Chock it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>How much power is that?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Enough for this beach and half the village.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We need about forty watts.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then you&apos;ll have a lot left over.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fathima holds out a tumbler of tea to Suresh. It is the first thing she has ever handed him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You came.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I brought the lights.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&apos;s what I said.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the far end of the beach, where the lanterns stop, a man in a clean shirt stands alone with his hands in his pockets. Joe. He walked here. He comes no closer.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fathima looks at him a long moment. Then she pours a second tumbler and sends Meenu down the beach with it. Joe takes it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to Meenu)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Thank her for me.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="38" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. THOMAS HOUSE - APPACHAN&apos;S ROOM - DUSK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali buttons Appachan&apos;s good white shirt, from the top, the right way. He is thinner since the hospital. The keys hang on their nail by the door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is she on tonight?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>മറിയാമ്മ. Is she on shift?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali&apos;s fingers stop on a button.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(a breath)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>ഉവ്വ്, അപ്പച്ചാ. She&apos;s on tonight. It&apos;s her last shift. We&apos;re all going down to the beach to listen to her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The sea?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Calm.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She&apos;ll be embarrassed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(smiling)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She&apos;ll laugh.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She always laughs.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He stands. Kannan gets up off the floor too. Appachan takes the keys from the nail and hangs them round his neck. He stands with them a moment. Then he lifts them off and hangs them back on the nail.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Leave them. We won&apos;t need them tonight.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="39" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. KADALKKARA BEACH - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text RevisionID="1">Rain has stopped. The generator truck hums behind the dunes. Three hundred people sit on the wet sand facing two speaker stacks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>AERIAL: THE BEACH IN THE DARK. A LONG CURVE OF LANTERNS STRUNG BETWEEN THE BOATS, THREE HUNDRED PEOPLE ON THE SAND FACING THE SEA, AND ABOVE THEM ON THE DUNE THE DEAD MAST.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The generator hums behind the boats. The two loudspeakers stand at the waterline facing inland, their backs to the sea. Families on mats. Children asleep on laps. The church choir sits together, not singing. Latha, in uniform, stands at the back by her jeep.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Appachan sits in a plastic chair in the front row, Kannan at his feet. Anjali on the sand beside him. Rahul and Meenu at the Nagra, headphones on.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali stands. The beach goes quiet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>My mother&apos;s name was Mariamma Thomas. Most of you knew her better than I did. I was eleven.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>This is the station&apos;s log tape from the fourteenth of November 1999. It runs fifty minutes. I haven&apos;t cut anything out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to Rahul)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Play it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Over the speakers, huge: hiss. Rain on a roof twenty-three years ago. Then Mariamma&apos;s laugh.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(over the speakers)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No, Joe, I&apos;m not going to say it like that, they&apos;ll think somebody died...</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Some of the old women laugh, they can&apos;t help it, and cover their mouths with the ends of their saris.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The tape runs. The tide creeps up the sand. Nobody leaves. On the tape, a heavy clunk, then a silence so complete that a child starts to cry and is hushed. Then, very small:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Little Flower, Little Flower, this is Kadalkkara, on battery.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VARGHESE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kadalkkara, Little Flower. Weak, but I have you. Go.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>In the front rows an old skipper stands up without meaning to. Then another.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Suresh stands and walks down to the speakers. At a nod from Anjali, Rahul brings the tape down low under him. It keeps running.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Eleven boats went out that night.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Little Flower. Seagull. Morning Star. Bismillah. Sea Queen. Ambadi. Jyothi. Mother Mary. St. Antony. Kairali. Pournami.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>With each name, somewhere in the crowd, somebody stands. An old man. A widow. A boy in his grandfather&apos;s place.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Eight came home. They came home on my father&apos;s light. My father had it from Mariamma Thomas, who sat in the dark and talked to him on a battery after the Port switched her off.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(he looks at Anjali)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>For twenty-three years we said the wrong thing about her. I said it loudest.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rahul brings the tape back up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VARGHESE (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;m going back for Hamza. Mariamma, I&apos;m going back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>By her urn, Fathima closes her eyes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the edge of the lantern light Joe stands where he has stood for an hour, on the point of leaving. Latha comes and stands beside him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(low)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Stay to the end.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JOE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(low)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I was here for the end once before. I left.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LATHA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>So stay this time.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He stays.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kadalkkara. Battery&apos;s going. Time is twenty-three fifty-one. Anjali&apos;s asleep.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Appachan turns his head toward the speakers, as if someone had called him from the next room.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(clearly, to no one)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There she is.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali takes his hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARIAMMA (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;ll put the lantern on the jetty for you, Varghese. Look for the lantern.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The long hiss.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(softly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Good girl. Go on now. Go home.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He is smiling. His eyes are wet. He is at peace.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fathima crouches beside Anjali.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Rahul&apos;s on the roof. He says it&apos;s ready.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali looks up at the dark station on the dune. A torch blinks twice on the roof.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="40" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. COAST RADIO STATION - ROOF - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A flat concrete roof, weeds in the cracks, the mast rising from one corner. Below: the lanterns, the people, the hum of the generator.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rahul kneels by the biscuit-tin transmitter on an upturned fish crate. A heavy cable runs from it to the base of the mast on crocodile clips. A car battery beside it. The Nagra, carried up in its suitcase, lies open next to the tin, the reel rewound.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali climbs up through the roof hatch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;ve matched it to the mast. It&apos;ll go ten kilometres. Fifteen if God&apos;s listening.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&apos;s not legal.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&apos;s not legal for one night.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He hands her the microphone: the Walkman microphone, taped to the handle of a wooden spoon.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What frequency?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hers. The one inside the front cover of the log.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She looks out at the sea, black, the lights of a few far boats on it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;ve never done this.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You do it every day. You just take it out afterwards.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She keys the microphone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into the microphone)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>All stations, all stations, this is Kadalkkara.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(her voice shakes; she lets it)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kadalkkara coast radio, for the last time from this mast. Wind northwest, ten knots. Swell under a metre. Low tide at five fourteen.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(a breath)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What follows is a recording from the night of the fourteenth of November 1999. The operator is Mariamma Thomas.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She nods. Rahul pushes the lever. The reels turn, the lamp on the tin glows, and Mariamma&apos;s voice goes out through the old mast into the dark.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They sit with their backs against the parapet. Below them, the beach. Ahead, the sea, listening.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>WIDE ON THE STATION FROM THE SEA: THE SMALL LIGHT ON THE ROOF, THE MAST, THE LANTERNS ALONG THE BEACH, ALL OF IT TINY UNDER THE STARS.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>DISSOLVE TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="41" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. COAST RADIO STATION - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Morning. Hard sun. A hundred people behind the tape, quieter than a crowd should be. Police barricades. Latha&apos;s jeep on the track.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A yellow tracked excavator with a demolition grab crawls up the sand track. Suresh, in a hard hat and a high-visibility vest, walks round it once and signals his crew to clear the area.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="General"><Text>[Note: Note to VFX: the mast fall is a composite. The practical excavator takes only the dressed false wall on the north face. The mast is a full CG replacement from the first lean, with practical dust and debris at the point of impact in the grove.]</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali stands at the tape with Appachan, in his good shirt again. Fathima on his other side. Kannan at their feet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What are they doing?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Taking it down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>APPACHAN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is everyone home?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everyone&apos;s home.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Suresh looks across. Anjali nods. He lifts his hand to the operator.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The arm rises, swings and bites into the first wall. Concrete groans and gives. Dust rolls out over the crowd. Fathima flinches. Appachan does not.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>By noon the operations room is a hill of broken concrete. The grab works at the mast footings. The steel shrieks. The mast leans, slowly, and seems to stop, as if thinking about it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Shot"><Text>WIDE ON THE MAST AS IT LEANS PAST THE POINT OF NO RETURN AND FALLS INTO THE COCONUT GROVE. PALM FRONDS BURST. CROWS GO UP IN A BLACK SHEET.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The ground shakes. Dust drifts over everything.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>In the silence, Appachan claps his hands. Once. The sharp clap the men on the beach give when a boat comes in over the bar.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>An old fisherman claps once too. Then a few more. Not applause. The clap for a boat coming in.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Suresh takes off his hard hat and walks over to the tape. He and Anjali stand on either side of it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SURESH</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&apos;s it, then.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&apos;s the building.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(holding out a tumbler of tea to Suresh)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Drink. You look like a ghost.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>CUT TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="42" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. FATHIMA&apos;S TEA SHOP - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The afternoon lull. Newspapers spread on the floor. Rahul kneels on them, painting white letters onto a long jackwood plank. Meenu outlines each one in blue. So far the plank says Low Tide Rad.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>On the counter the biscuit-tin transmitter, scorched black down one side, has a cardboard sign in Meenu&apos;s writing: Do not touch. Historic.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali sits at the corner table with her phone. Fathima wipes the same patch of counter for a long time.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>STUDIO PRODUCER (ON PHONE)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;ve held the detergent people a week, Anjali. They redid the voice. She sounds tired again. I need you back Monday.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&apos;m not coming back, Nikhil. I&apos;ll send notes on the open sessions. Tell the client she sounds tired because she&apos;s a person.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>STUDIO PRODUCER (ON PHONE)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is this about money? Because I can...</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&apos;s about breathing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Leave the breaths in. That&apos;s my last note.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She hangs up and puts the phone face down. Fathima keeps wiping.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You can stop pretending you weren&apos;t listening.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I wasn&apos;t listening. I&apos;m cleaning.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That spot&apos;s very clean.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FATHIMA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&apos;s a very dirty spot.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>So you&apos;re staying.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Somebody has to stop him setting the shop on fire.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(not looking up)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Once. Small fire.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fathima puts the cloth down, comes round the counter, sits across from Anjali and says nothing at all, which for Fathima is a speech.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rahul paints the last letter and sits back on his heels.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Low Tide Radio.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MEENU</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(critically)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The O is fat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAHUL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The O is perfect.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="43" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. KADALKKARA HARBOUR - DAWN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Blue light. Engines cough and catch one after another down the wharf. Diesel smoke on the water. Men throw lines and jump aboard.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>In every wheelhouse, a radio. Cheap transistors on shelves. A marine set on the Bismillah. A transistor hung from a nail on the Jyothi. From all of them at once, a little out of step, the same voice.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI (ON RADIO)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Good morning, Kadalkkara. This is Low Tide Radio with the weather for the boats. Wind east-northeast, eight knots, backing north by afternoon. Swell one metre. Visibility good.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>On the Jyothi the Old Fisherman stops coiling a rope and turns the transistor up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI (ON RADIO) (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Low tide at five forty...</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(a rustle of paper)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No. Five fifty-two. Sorry, Raghavan. Don&apos;t come and shout at me.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The Old Fisherman laughs out loud, alone on his deck. On the radio Anjali laughs too. She lets it run. Nobody cuts it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ANJALI (ON RADIO) (CONT&apos;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(still laughing)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Go well. Come home.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The boats go out through the harbour mouth in a line, past the breakwater, into the light.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Number="44" Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. KADALKKARA BEACH - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Low tide. The sea has gone out a very long way. The wet sand shines like a mirror with the sky in it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali and Appachan walk along the water&apos;s edge, slowly, his arm through hers. Kannan runs ahead through the shallow pools, scattering crabs, runs back, runs ahead again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Behind them, on the dune where the station stood, there is only sky, a heap of broken concrete and a stump of steel.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Anjali stops and looks at the watch on her wrist. 23:52. She pulls out the crown and winds it, turn after turn, then sets the hands to the morning. The second hand starts to move.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>When she takes his arm again, Appachan hums. Four notes, wobbling. The station jingle from the cassette.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She hums it back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They walk on. Behind them, their footprints fill with water.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>FADE OUT.</Text></Paragraph>
</Content>
